# Everything is stored with LF in the repository, whatever a clone's
# `core.autocrlf` happens to be. This normalises the INDEX only — working
# trees keep the platform's own convention, so no clone gets rewritten.
#
# Not housekeeping: `account-merge.service.ts` reached `main` with CRLF and
# went unnoticed, and a file whose stored endings differ from its neighbours'
# shows up as wholly rewritten the first time anyone edits it on a machine
# configured the other way — a diff nobody can review.
#
# Kept first so every rule below still wins where line endings are content
# rather than convention.
* text=auto

# Force LF for shell scripts even on Windows clones — they end up inside
# Linux containers where CRLF causes "no such file or directory" from
# `exec` (a notorious Docker pitfall).
*.sh        text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf

# Treat SVG and JSON deterministically so diffs stay tidy.
*.svg       text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf

# The vendored landing kit is content-addressed: `landing-kit.manifest.json`
# stores a sha256 per file and a test fails the build on any mismatch. Line
# endings are content to a hash, so without this pin the manifest would be
# valid only on the machine that generated it — red on CI and on every fresh
# Windows clone, for files nobody edited.
rezeis-admin/web/src/features/landing-builder/live/** text eol=lf
